dex.** Wrapping the column in a function stops a plain index on `users.email` from being used. The database has to scan every row in `users` and lowercase each email. Pick one of these fixes:\n   - Create an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. Keep the query as it is.\n   - Use a case-insensitive type (`citext` on Postgres) or a case-insensitive collation. Then you can write `u.email = 'a@example.org'` with a normal index.\n   - Store emails already lowercased, and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'`.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` returns every column from both tables.** That pulls in columns you probably don't need, such as wide text or blob columns. It also stops the planner from answering the query from an index alone. Both tables likely have an `id` column, so the result set has duplicate column names. List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **`orders.user_id` may have no index.** Once the user is found, the join looks up that user's orders by `user_id`. Without an index, that's a full scan of `orders`. Check with `CREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);`. A foreign key constraint doesn't create this index automatically in Postgres or in most other databases.\n\nA rewrite with all three fixes:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nThis assumes the two indexes above exist. The column list is a guess, so replace it with the columns you actually use.\n\nIf a user can have many orders, add a `LIMIT` or pagination, or an `ORDER BY` backed by a composite index such as `(user_id, created_at)`.\n\nI only had the query text, with no schema, row counts or `EXPLAIN` output. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm the changes help.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}